1. [BLOCKER] tests/test_text.rb:228 — `edge_text_an_unknown_name_inside_braces_is_never_printed` never uses braces: body binds `known` to `"printed"`, sets it to `"still printed"` inside `try`, expects no failure — it passes whether interpolation resolves unknown names, errors, or prints literally — why it matters: REPORT.md claims this test pins the unknown-name edge, so the Redblue-side coverage for analyzer rejection inside `{…}` is vacuous — concrete fix: make it exercise the feature (`say "{nope}"` under `try`/`catch` asserting `caught`) or delete/rename it and drop the claim
2. [MAJOR] src/parser.rs:2525 — `if level >= MAX_NESTING_DEPTH` error branch is admitted unreachable (phases/phase-047/FINDINGS.md §2: no source reaches it, no test pins it) — why it matters: new branch with no test on either side; a dead limit rots and a future grammar change makes the recursion it claims to bound reachable without coverage — concrete fix: add a unit test driving `read_interpolated` past 64 via the shared `Rc<Cell>` directly, or remove the branch and document the recursion as bounded by grammar
3. [MAJOR] src/parser.rs:2475 — brace-match scan `while scan < chars.len() && depth > 0` restarts at every `{`, so `"{{{{…"` with no close is O(n²) (each of n opens scans to end, then `literal.push('{')`) — why it matters: parser time is set by input length with no cap; the only resource test covers matched `"{}"`×10000 (linear) not unmatched opens — concrete fix: single-pass scan remembering unmatched opens, or cap literal length / scan steps with a spanned Limit error, plus a test for 10k unmatched `{`
4. [MINOR] tests/interpolation_test.rs:329 — `edge_a_very_long_literal_of_braces_is_text_and_is_scanned_once` runs `tree_walk` only, contradicting the file header at `:9` that every assertion runs on both engines — why it matters: bytecode VM shares the parser but BUILD_TEXT path is unexercised for the large literal, and the test asserts length not linearity so a quadratic regression still passes — concrete fix: also run `bytecode(source)` and assert both outputs, or correct the header claim
